# Where Unit 1 Fits

<p>Semester 1 of Building AI Systems is the foundation for Semester 2, when students work in Microsoft Foundry and prepare for the AI-901 certification. </p>
  
<p>Unit 1 is where that foundation starts. Here's what students carry forward:</p>

<p>• <b>Python fluency.</b> Students write Python in every unit of Semester 1, so they arrive in Semester 2 ready to use it in Foundry without also learning the language.</p>

<p>• <b>Reasoning about different approaches to AI.</b> In Unit 1, students build rule-based systems and see where they break down. That's a light first pass at the concepts Domain 1 covers later.</p>

<p>• <b>Debugging habits.</b> Students start practicing in Unit 1 how to read an error, test a small change, and try again. They'll lean on those habits all year, especially once they're troubleshooting real Foundry implementations.</p>

<div style="background-color: #D5EFFF; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #06338D; line-height: 1.5;">
    <strong>The takeaway:</strong> if Unit 1 feels disconnected from AI-901, that's expected. You're building foundations here, not chasing exam content. You'll see the certification framing get more direct as the units go on and the content starts mapping onto tested domains.
  </div>
</div>

<hr>

